    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03329-x",
    "abstract": "Reforestation is considered an important nature-based climate solution to help achieve net-zero CO2 emissions. However, strategies using reforestation-based CO2 removal to offset fossil fuel emissions may not lead to the same climate outcome as avoiding the fossil fuel emissions. Here, we use an Earth System model of intermediate complexity to compare the climate outcome of different pathways: a reference pathway, and net-zero pathways where additional fossil fuel CO2 emissions relative to the reference pathway are balanced by reforestation-based CO2 removals (\u201cReforestation Net-zero pathways\u201d). Results show that model simulations of Reforestation Net-zero pathways yield a higher atmospheric CO2 and warmer climate outcome than the reference simulation. The higher atmospheric CO2 results from carbon cycle feedbacks. The additional global warming from higher atmospheric CO2 is further amplified by biogeophysical effects of reforestation. These findings highlight the need for improved methods to account for the carbon cycle and climate effects of reforestation. Higher atmospheric CO2 and warmer climate are found in net-zero pathways that balance fossil fuel emissions with reforestation-based removals compared to an idealized mitigation pathway, as revealed by scenario simulations with an Earth system model.",
    "field": "Climate change mitigation",
    "problem": "This paper addresses why balancing fossil fuel CO\u2082 emissions with reforestation-based removals leads to a warmer climate than avoiding the emissions, a critical imbalance caused by overlooked carbon cycle feedbacks and biogeophysical effects of reforestation.",
    "method": "This study uses an Earth System model to compare the climate outcomes of a reference zero-emission pathway with net-zero pathways where additional fossil fuel CO2 emissions are balanced by CO2 removals from global and sustainable reforestation scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to compare the climate outcomes of a net-zero pathway that balances fossil fuel CO\u2082 emissions with reforestation-based CO\u2082 removals against a reference pathway where the additional fossil fuel emissions are avoided, using an Earth System model.",
